window.__labLoaded({"id":"6.5","title":"The One-Sample t-Test","goal":"Check whether the average nitrate level in a river differs from the 3.2 mg/L guideline, using the lesson's nine readings.","question":"An environmental agency has a guideline of 3.2 mg/L for nitrate in river water. A technician takes nine water samples from one river and measures nitrate in each. Does the river's true average level differ from the guideline, or is the gap between the sample mean and 3.2 just the luck of which nine samples we took?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Enter the data","text":"H0: the river's true mean nitrate is 3.2 mg/L. H1: it is not 3.2 (higher or lower). We have nine readings in mg/L and the guideline value. NumPy holds the numbers and SciPy supplies the test.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnitrate = np.array([3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5])\nguideline = 3.2\n\nprint('readings:', len(nitrate))","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, under the short name np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools, which contain the t-test we need.","Store the nine readings as an array so we can do arithmetic on all of them at once.","Store the guideline under a name, so the value we compare with is easy to find and change.","Print the number of readings to confirm all nine were stored."],"meaning":"Nine readings are stored and the guideline is 3.2 mg/L. Nothing has been tested yet. The setup matters because the t-test needs the whole sample, not just its mean: the sample size and spread decide how much a mean can be trusted.","out":"readings: 9","figs":[]},{"title":"Describe the sample","text":"Always look at the numbers before testing them. The mean is the sample's centre, the standard deviation (ddof=1, the sample version) is its typical spread, and the standard error says how much a mean of 9 readings wobbles.","code":"mean = nitrate.mean()\nsd = nitrate.std(ddof=1)\nse = sd / np.sqrt(len(nitrate))\n\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.3f}')\nprint(f'sd   = {sd:.3f}')\nprint(f'se   = {se:.3f}')","explain":["The sample mean: the centre of the nine readings, our best guess for the river's true mean.","The sample SD (ddof=1 means divide by n - 1): how far a typical single reading is from the mean.","The standard error: the SD divided by the square root of n. It says how much a MEAN of nine readings would wobble.","Print the mean to 3 decimals.","Print the SD to 3 decimals.","Print the standard error to 3 decimals."],"meaning":"The mean is 3.678, about 0.48 above the guideline. The SD is 0.554, so single readings typically vary by half a mg/L. The standard error is 0.185: a mean of nine readings wobbles far less than one reading does. The question becomes how many standard errors 3.678 is from 3.2.","out":"mean = 3.678\nsd   = 0.554\nse   = 0.185","figs":[]},{"title":"Run the one-sample t-test","text":"ttest_1samp asks: if the true average were exactly 3.2, how surprising is a sample mean this far away? It returns the t statistic (distance in standard errors) and the two-sided p-value.","code":"result = stats.ttest_1samp(nitrate, popmean=guideline)\n\nprint(f't  = {result.statistic:.3f}')\nprint(f'df = {len(nitrate) - 1}')\nprint(f'p  = {result.pvalue:.4f}')","explain":["Run the one-sample t-test: is the mean of nitrate different from popmean = 3.2? It uses the t curve because we estimated the SD.","Print t, the distance between mean and guideline measured in standard errors.","Print the degrees of freedom, n - 1 = 8, which sets the exact shape of the t curve.","Print the two-sided p-value to 4 decimals."],"meaning":"t = 2.587: our mean is about 2.6 standard errors above 3.2. With df = 8 the p-value is 0.0323. If the true mean were 3.2, a sample mean this far away (in either direction) would turn up about 3% of the time. That is below 0.05, so we reject H0. It is not the chance that H0 is true.","out":"t  = 2.587\ndf = 8\np  = 0.0323","figs":[]},{"title":"Add a confidence interval","text":"A 95% interval shows the range of true averages that fit the data. The guideline sits outside it, which agrees with the small p-value.","code":"ci = result.confidence_interval(confidence_level=0.95)\n\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci.low:.3f}, {ci.high:.3f})')\nprint('guideline inside the interval?', ci.low <= guideline <= ci.high)","explain":["Ask the test result for a 95% confidence interval for the true mean river level.","Print the interval ends, rounded to 3 decimals.","Check whether the guideline lies inside the interval. This prints True or False."],"meaning":"The 95% interval for the river's true mean is 3.252 to 4.104. The guideline 3.2 is outside it (False), which agrees with p < 0.05. The interval also tells us how far: plausibly 0.05 to 0.9 mg/L above the guideline. It is wide because we have only nine readings.","out":"95% CI: (3.252, 4.104)\nguideline inside the interval? False","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"nitrate <- c(3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5)\nt.test(nitrate, mu = 3.2)","explain":["Store the nine readings in a vector called nitrate. c() combines values and <- assigns them to a name.","t.test with mu = 3.2 runs the one-sample t-test against the guideline and prints t, df, the p-value and a 95% interval."],"expect":"t = 2.5866, df = 8, p-value = 0.03226; 95 percent confidence interval: 3.252 to 4.104; sample mean 3.678","read":"Look at t = 2.5866, df = 8, p-value = 0.03226: below 0.05, so we reject H0. The 95 percent confidence interval, 3.252 to 4.104, excludes 3.2. The last line shows the sample mean, 3.678.","note":"t.test prints the t statistic, degrees of freedom, p-value and the 95% confidence interval together."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Compare Means > One-Sample T Test","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / nitrate.\nBEGIN DATA\n3.1 4.2 2.8 3.9 4.5 3.3 4.1 3.7 3.5\nEND DATA.\nT-TEST\n  /TESTVAL=3.2\n  /VARIABLES=nitrate\n  /CRITERIA=CI(.95).","explain":["Define one variable, nitrate, read in free format.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","The nine readings on one line, separated by spaces.","Marks the end of the data.","T-TEST starts SPSS's t-test command.","/TESTVAL=3.2 sets the value we compare the mean with, our guideline.","/VARIABLES=nitrate says which variable to test.","/CRITERIA=CI(.95) asks for a 95% confidence interval. The period ends the command."],"read":"In the One-Sample Test table look at t (2.587), df (8) and Sig. (2-tailed) (0.032): below 0.05, so reject H0. The mean difference is 0.478 (3.678 minus 3.2) and its 95% interval runs from 0.052 to 0.904.","note":"In the One-Sample Test table read t, df and Sig. (2-tailed); the confidence interval is for the difference from 3.2."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput nitrate\n3.1\n4.2\n2.8\n3.9\n4.5\n3.3\n4.1\n3.7\n3.5\nend\nttest nitrate == 3.2","explain":["clear removes any data in memory so we start fresh.","input starts typing data directly; nitrate is the name of the one variable.","Reading 1: 3.1 mg/L.","Reading 2: 4.2 mg/L.","Reading 3: 2.8 mg/L.","Reading 4: 3.9 mg/L.","Reading 5: 4.5 mg/L.","Reading 6: 3.3 mg/L.","Reading 7: 4.1 mg/L.","Reading 8: 3.7 mg/L.","Reading 9: 3.5 mg/L.","end finishes data entry.","ttest nitrate == 3.2 runs the one-sample t-test of the mean against 3.2."],"read":"Look for the mean 3.677778 and its 95% confidence interval (3.252 to 4.104), then t = 2.5866 with 8 degrees of freedom. The middle line, Pr(|T| > |t|) = 0.0323, is the two-sided p-value, below 0.05, so reject H0.","note":"ttest reports the mean, its 95% confidence interval, t, degrees of freedom and the two-sided p-value (the middle Pr(|T| > |t|) line)."},"conclusion":"The river's average nitrate level is estimated at 3.68 mg/L, and the one-sample t-test (t = 2.587, p = 0.0323) says it differs from the 3.2 guideline. The 95% interval, 3.25 to 4.10, lies entirely above 3.2. Caution: nine readings give a wide interval, the test assumes independent, roughly normal readings, and it cannot say why the level is high.","practice":{"scenario":"A bakery says its loaves weigh 450 g on average. A food inspector buys 10 loaves at random and weighs each one. Does the true average weight differ from 450 g? Run a one-sample t-test and report a 95% confidence interval for the true mean.","data":"grams = [442.3, 421.3, 439.2, 443.6, 445.0, 430.2, 439.7, 435.2, 436.7, 453.5]","tasks":["Write H0 and H1, then find the sample mean and SD.","Run the one-sample t-test against 450 g and report t, df and p.","Find the 95% confidence interval for the true mean.","Decide at alpha = 0.05 and say whether the bakery's claim holds."],"hint":"This is the nitrate lab with new numbers: stats.ttest_1samp(grams, popmean=450), then use .confidence_interval(0.95) on the result.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ngrams = np.array([442.3, 421.3, 439.2, 443.6, 445.0, 430.2, 439.7, 435.2, 436.7, 453.5])\nres = stats.ttest_1samp(grams, popmean=450)\nci = res.confidence_interval(0.95)\nprint(f'mean = {grams.mean():.2f}, sd = {grams.std(ddof=1):.2f}')\nprint(f't = {res.statistic:.3f}, df = {len(grams) - 1}, p = {res.pvalue:.5f}')\nprint(f'95% CI: ({ci.low:.2f}, {ci.high:.2f})')","answer":"The mean is 438.67 g and the SD is 8.74 g. t = -4.097 with df = 9 and p = 0.00269, far below 0.05, so we reject H0: the average loaf does not weigh 450 g. The 95% interval, 432.41 to 444.93 g, excludes 450, so loaves are plausibly 5 to 18 g light. With only 10 loaves from one bakery this is a sample, not a full audit.","out":"mean = 438.67, sd = 8.74\nt = -4.097, df = 9, p = 0.00269\n95% CI: (432.41, 444.93)","figs":[]}}});
